.TH FSTL 1 "June 2026" "fstl 0.13.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fstl \- fast viewer and exporter for .stl 3D model files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fstl
[\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-foreground\fR]
[\fIfile.stl\fR]
.br
.B fstl
\fIinputs\fR
\fB\-\-export\-png\fR|\fB\-\-export\-gif\fR
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fstl
is a very fast viewer for STL (stereolithography) 3D model files,
designed to load and render very high-polygon models instantly \-
2 million triangles at 60+ FPS on a mid-range laptop.
.PP
Launched without export switches it opens a GUI viewer with arcball
rotation (left-drag), panning (right-drag), zoom about the cursor
(scroll wheel), keyboard movement (rebindable via View > Configure
Keyboard Shortcuts), optional momentum spin (a released drag keeps the
model rotating), four draw modes, standard viewpoints (with a Z-up/Y-up toggle under
View > Up Axis for models authored either way), and automatic reload
when the file changes on disk.
.PP
GUI exporters cover screenshots, rotation PNG series, animated
turntable GIFs, and rotation MP4 videos with per-axis degree totals
(View > Animate Rotation additionally plays and live-records continuous
rotation with per-axis speeds and optional randomization). MP4 features
require
.BR ffmpeg (1)
on the PATH.
.PP
With
.B \-\-export\-png
and/or
.BR \-\-export\-gif ,
fstl runs as a command-line renderer with no window: it reads STL files
(or stdin) and writes PNG images or animated GIFs, with configurable
model color, background (transparent by default), resolution, and
rotation.
.SH GUI OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-foreground
Stay attached to the launching terminal. By default the GUI forks into
the background so the shell prompt returns immediately and the viewer
survives the terminal closing.
.TP
.I file.stl
The model to open. A leading
.B ~
expands to the home directory. Without a file, a demo sphere is shown.
.SH EXPORT MODE SELECTION
.TP
.B \-\-export\-png
Export PNG image(s); one image per angle given in
.BR \-\-angles .
.TP
.B \-\-export\-gif
Export an animated GIF of the model rotating. May be combined with the
other export switches in a single run.
.TP
.B \-\-export\-mp4
Export an MP4 video of the model rotating (requires
.BR ffmpeg (1)
on the PATH). Duration and per-axis rotation totals come from
.BR \-\-duration ", " \-\-rx ", " \-\-ry ", " \-\-rz .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage information and exit (never opens the GUI).
.TP
.B \-\-help\-all
Like
.BR \-\-help ,
but also lists Qt's own command-line options.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH INPUT AND OUTPUT OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-i ", " \-\-input " file"
Input .stl file; may be repeated. Positional arguments work the same
way. Passing
.B \-
reads STL data (binary or ASCII) from standard input, so fstl can sit
at the end of a pipeline.
.TP
.BI \-\-input\-dir " dir"
Render every .stl file in the directory (sorted), in addition to any
other inputs.
.TP
.BI \-o ", " \-\-output " name"
Output file name; the correct .png/.gif extension is added if missing.
Only valid with a single input \- use
.B \-\-output\-dir
for batches. Default: the input file's base name.
.TP
.BI \-\-output\-dir " dir"
Directory for output files. Default: alongside the input file (the
current directory for stdin input).
.PP
With several PNG angles, files are numbered
.IR name_000_0deg.png ,
.IR name_001_45deg.png ,
and so on.
.SH APPEARANCE OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-color " color"
Model color: a common (SVG/CSS) color name such as
.IR red ", " steelblue ", " goldenrod ,
or a hex value
.IR #rgb ", " #rrggbb ", " #aarrggbb .
Default: white.
.TP
.BI \-\-bg " color"
Background color, same formats. Default: transparent. Any background
with alpha below 255 produces a transparent background. GIF
transparency is 1\-bit (each pixel fully opaque or fully invisible), so
transparent GIFs have harder edges than PNGs.
.TP
.BI \-\-opacity " value"
Model opacity, 0..1 (default 1).
.TP
.BI \-\-brightness " value"
Light brightness multiplier (default 1).
.TP
.BI \-\-light\-dir " x,y,z"
Light direction vector (default 0,-0.5,1).
.TP
.BI \-\-settings " file"
Read appearance defaults (model/background colors, opacity, brightness,
light direction) from a settings .ini exported via File > Export
Settings in the GUI; explicit switches override. Ideal for styling
batch pipelines consistently.
.TP
.BI \-\-duration " s"
MP4: video length in seconds (default 8).
.TP
.BI \-\-rx " deg" ", " \-\-ry " deg" ", " \-\-rz " deg"
MP4: total rotation per axis, each may exceed 360 degrees (defaults
0, 360, 0); per-axis speed follows from degrees/duration.
.SH RESOLUTION OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-width " px" ", " \-\-height " px"
Maximum output size. Defaults: 1024 for PNG, 640 for GIF. The output
always preserves the model's projected aspect ratio: the image is
fitted inside the limits, sized so the model (over all exported angles)
fills the frame with a small margin, centered on x and y. Giving only
one limit applies it to both dimensions.
.SH ROTATION OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-axis " x\fR|\fPy\fR|\fPz"
Rotation axis in screen space:
.B y
= vertical (turntable, default),
.B x
= horizontal (tumble),
.B z
= screen normal (roll).
.TP
.BI \-\-angles " list"
PNG: comma-separated rotation angles in degrees, e.g.
.IR 0,45,90 .
Default: 0 (a straight-on front view).
.TP
.BI \-\-sweep " deg"
GIF loop mode: total rotation, up to 360 degrees (default 360). The
animation loops forever.
.TP
.B \-\-bounce
GIF: bounce back and forth between
.B \-\-from
and
.B \-\-to
instead of looping in one direction.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " deg" ", " \-\-to " deg"
Bounce endpoints in degrees (defaults \-90 and 90).
.TP
.BI \-\-step " deg"
Degrees of rotation per GIF frame (default 3; 360/3 = 120 frames).
.TP
.BI \-\-fps " fps"
GIF playback speed in frames per second (default 25).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Open a model in the viewer:
.B fstl model.stl
.TP
Straight-on transparent PNG, white model, max 1024 px:
.B fstl model.stl \-\-export\-png
.TP
Full 360\(de turntable GIF, transparent background, max 640 px:
.B fstl model.stl \-\-export\-gif
.TP
Bounce \-90\(de..+90\(de, green model on white, 200 px wide:
.B fstl model.stl \-\-export\-gif \-\-bounce \-\-from \-90 \-\-to 90 \-\-color '#00ff88' \-\-bg white \-\-width 200
.TP
Four PNG views in one run:
.B fstl model.stl \-\-export\-png \-\-angles '0,45,90,135'
.TP
Batch a directory; read from a pipeline:
.B fstl \-\-input\-dir ./models \-\-export\-png \-\-output\-dir ./renders
.br
.B cat model.stl | fstl \- \-\-export\-png \-\-output render.png
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
At least one input failed to load or render.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid arguments.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Exports need working OpenGL. With no display available fstl falls back
to Qt's
.B offscreen
platform; if that cannot create a GL context (no EGL), run through
.BR xvfb-run (1).
.B QT_QPA_PLATFORM
overrides the Qt platform plugin as usual.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xvfb-run (1)
.PP
Project page:
.I https://github.com/fstl-app/fstl
.SH AUTHOR
Originally written by Matt Keeter; maintained by the fstl-app
contributors.
